  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "The Daric was one of the most famous gold coins of the ancient world,
    introduced during the Achaemenid Persian Empire, most likely under Darius I (c.
    522\u2013486 BCE). Weighing approximately 8.4 grams and made of high-quality
    gold, the coin is traditionally believed to have taken its name from Darius the
    Great. Its distinctive design depicts the Persian king as a kneeling-running
    archer, armed with a bow and a spear or arrow, symbolizing the military strength
    and royal authority of the Achaemenid Empire.\nThe Daric became a widely respected
    currency throughout the ancient Near East and beyond. It was used for military
    payments, royal expenditures, and international trade, helping facilitate economic
    activity across the vast Persian Empire. Its consistent weight and high gold
    content contributed to its reputation and recognition among neighboring civilizations,
    including the Greeks. More than a means of exchange, the Daric represented the
    wealth, administrative sophistication, and far-reaching influence of the Achaemenid
    Empire, which extended across vast territories from Central Asia to Asia Minor
    and Egypt."
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 7308c1d742b3a46bbb0f12fa01af6bb5, type: 2}
