  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "Achaemenid soldier reliefs are among the most remarkable examples of ancient
    Persian art, dating to the Achaemenid Empire (c. 550\u2013330 BCE). Particularly
    prominent at Persepolis, these carvings depict soldiers, royal guards, and representatives
    of the diverse peoples who formed part of the empire. Their detailed representations
    of clothing, headgear, weapons, and military equipment provide valuable insight
    into Achaemenid dress, warfare, and artistic conventions. These reliefs were
    part of the empire\u2019s official visual culture, expressing royal authority,
    discipline, and the diversity of the Persian realm.\nThe Achaemenid army was
    a highly organized force that incorporated troops from numerous regions and ethnic
    groups across the empire. Among its best-known military associations is the Immortals,
    an elite royal force described by ancient Greek historians. Reliefs featuring
    armed guards and spear-bearing figures help illustrate the ceremonial and military
    traditions of the Persian court. Beyond their artistic beauty, these monuments
    serve as important historical sources, offering insight into the military organization,
    cultural identity, and political power of one of the ancient world\u2019s most
    influential empires."
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 7308c1d742b3a46bbb0f12fa01af6bb5, type: 2}
