  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "Cyrus the Great, the founder of the Achaemenid Persian Empire in the sixth
    century BCE, is renowned for establishing one of the largest empires of the ancient
    world. Although rock reliefs played an important role in ancient Persian art
    and royal representation, no securely identified and widely accepted rock relief
    depicting Cyrus himself has been conclusively established. Consequently, modern
    images presented as portraits of Cyrus may represent artistic reconstructions
    rather than historically verified depictions of the king.\nThe archaeological
    remains of Pasargadae, in present-day Fars Province, Iran, provide some of the
    most important evidence of Cyrus\u2019s reign and the early Achaemenid artistic
    tradition. The site includes his traditionally identified tomb and architectural
    remains that reflect the cultural influences of the emerging Persian Empire.
    Achaemenid art incorporated artistic traditions from different regions under
    Persian rule, helping create a distinctive imperial visual identity. Studying
    these monuments offers valuable insight into Cyrus\u2019s historical legacy,
    early Persian architecture, and the cultural heritage of the Achaemenid Empire."
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 7308c1d742b3a46bbb0f12fa01af6bb5, type: 2}
